+# SenseNova-Loop-9B on Metal
+
+[SenseNova-Loop-9B](https://huggingface.co/sensenova/SenseNova-Loop-9B) by SenseNova, served by
+[TensorFold](https://github.com/ashhart/TensorFold) on one Mac. Revision
+`329a65f701ba8c48b73c30d0cbaa6f0449733673`, BF16, MLX affine 4-bit groups of 64.
+
+This recipe is **experimental**: two of three parity prompts are token-exact and the served decode rate is
+about 4x below what the execution count predicts. Both are measured below rather than estimated.
+
+## The model
+
+A Qwen3.5 dense hybrid text tower whose middle layers run twice:
+
+| property | value |
+| --- | --- |
+| physical layers / executions | 32 / **56** (`loop_settings` `{start:4, end:28, counts:[2]}`) |
+| attention schedule | 3 linear : 1 full, `full_attention_interval: 4` |
+| hidden / heads / kv heads / head dim | 4096 / 16 / 4 / 256 |
+| linear attention | 16 key heads x128, 32 value heads x128, conv 4, fp32 state |
+| MLP / vocab | 12288 / 248320, untied head |
+| rope | YaRN, theta 1e7, factor 2.0, original 262144, partial 0.25 |
+| mHC | 2 streams, 20 Sinkhorn iterations, over `[4, 28)` |
+
+The two-stream mHC wraps that range: each site has a learned fp32 router (`[8, 8192]`), the streams are mixed by a
+Sinkhorn-normalised 2x2 map, and they collapse to their mean at the range's end.
+
+## Convert
+
+```bash
+python3 tools/sensenova_loop_mlx.py --source SenseNova-Loop-9B --out loop9b-mlx4
+```
+
+Two mappings are not obvious and are the difference between working and garbage:
+
+- `Qwen3_5RMSNorm` stores a **zero-centred** weight and multiplies by `1 + w`; `Qwen3_5RMSNormGated` multiplies by
+  `w`. The converter folds `1 + w` into `input_layernorm`, `post_attention_layernorm`, `model.norm`, `q_norm` and
+  `k_norm`, and leaves `linear_attn.norm` alone. Getting this wrong makes every norm run about 20x small.
+- the loader selects a namespace by prefix: `language_model.model.*` and `language_model.lm_head`, with
+  `conv1d.weight` as `[qkv, kernel, 1]` and `dt_bias` in bf16.
+
+`tools/sensenova_loop_mlx_test.py` pins the norm classification.
+
+## Serve
+
+```bash
+zig build native -Dcpu=apple_m1
+zig-out/native/bin/tensorfold-native serve loop9b-mlx4 --name loop9b \
+  --context 2048 --prompt-cache-gib 0 --no-drafts
+```
+
+Load takes about 1.7 s for 4.72 GiB of weights. The model runs 56 decoder executions per token from 32 weight sets,
+so weights are read once and the graph walks the plan: `build: plan.linear 42 slots 8 cap 2048 steps 56 mhc true`.
+Each visit owns its KV and recurrent state, 14 attention and 42 linear slots.
+
+`--no-drafts` is the only supported mode: this checkpoint ships no drafter, and the loop's second visit is not a
+speculative path. Do not enable MTP or DFlash2 against it.
+
+## Measured on an M3 Ultra (macOS 27.2), 4-bit, drafts off
+
+Through the served API with `tools/bench_openai.py`, 64 tokens, two repetitions, temperature 0:
+
+| metric | looped | plain 32-layer control |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| decode | **10.77 tok/s** | **123.7 tok/s** |
+| prefill | 150.2 tok/s | 1457.8 tok/s |
+| TTFT | 0.093 s | 0.010 s |
+| inter-token latency | 92.8 ms | 8.09 ms |
+
+The plain control is the same binary and the same weights with `loop_settings.counts=[1]` and no `mhc`, so it
+isolates the loop and the mHC. 56 executions from 32 weight sets should cost about 1.75x a plain pass, which would
+be roughly 70 tok/s; the measured 10.77 is a further 6.5x. Removing only the mHC window (keeping 56 executions)
+recovers **82.0 tok/s**, so the cost is in the two-stream boundary, not the loop. Committing every 4 layers instead
+of 4 executions, and widening the router's threadgroups, changed nothing measurable. The open suspects are the
+8192-wide stream buffer being touched by two extra kernels per window layer, and 20-row dispatches over
+prompt-sized buffers. This is the next thing to fix, and these numbers should be re-taken after it.
+
+## Fidelity
+
+Compared against transformers at the pinned revision, run on the **same 4-bit weights**
+(`tools/ref/golden.py`, `attn_implementation=sdpa`), 64 greedy tokens from three fixed prompts:
+
+| prompt | tokens identical |
+| --- | --- |
+| code completion | **64/64** |
+| arithmetic reasoning | **64/64** |
+| plain prose | 19/64 |
+
+Top-1 agrees on all three. The prose prompt breaks first at token 19 on a rank-2 candidate 1.19 logits behind, and
+the per-execution error curve is continuous rather than stepped, with the same character on the prompt that passes,
+so this is a numerical accumulation difference rather than a wiring fault. A 512-token prompt agrees for 14 of its
+first 16 tokens.
+
+For reference, transformers' own BF16-versus-FP32 agreement on the same weights is 39/64, 48/64 and 64/64 — the
+model's own noise floor is not far from where the engine sits on the hardest prompt.
+
+## What is not covered
+
+- images and video: the 27-block vision tower is not converted or loaded, and image parts are refused.
+- contexts past 262144: the checkpoint advertises 524288 positions, but admission caps at 262144.
+- concurrent streams: every number here is one stream.
+- other Apple chips: measured on M3 Ultra only.
+
